---
import GuideLayout from '../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { GAME_IMAGES } from '../lib/images';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Beginner’s guide', path: '/beginner-guide/' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'before', label: 'Before you start' },
  { id: 'opening', label: 'The opening route' },
  { id: 'station', label: 'Station power and belts' },
  { id: 'research', label: 'Research priorities' },
  { id: 'tips', label: 'Field tips' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Common mistakes' },
  { id: 'launch', label: 'Demo vs Early Access' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Beginner questions' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Is this beginner guide based on the full game?',
    answer:
      'No. It is based on the playable demo, official developer posts and storefront descriptions, with player-tested findings labelled as such. The Early Access build may change recipes, research costs and tutorial flow.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why can’t I find conveyor belts in the build menu?',
    answer:
      'In the current demo, conveyor belts are gated behind the Logistics I research step. You need a powered station and a working science/research input before the conveyor build option appears. The dedicated conveyor belt guide covers the blockage step by step.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should I research first?',
    answer:
      'The visible demo tree places Logistics I before Mining Drones and Computation Science. Power technology is a separate branch. In practice, secure enough station power to run research and machines before expanding production. The game does not publish a single mandatory order for every playstyle.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need to play the demo before Early Access?',
    answer:
      'You do not have to, but it is the best way to see whether the first-person building and factory flow suit you. The demo is free, and its progress is not confirmed to carry into Early Access.',
  },
];

const block = 'scroll-mt-28';
---

<GuideLayout
  seoTitle="Main Sequence Beginner's Guide: First Station, Power & Research"
  description="A practical Main Sequence beginner's guide: scan and mine, refine aboard your ship, power a station, unlock Logistics I and start your first automated production loop."
  eyebrow="Start here"
  title="Main Sequence beginner's guide: from mining ship to working factory"
  intro="A spoiler-light route through the demo opening, including the two steps that most often stop new commanders: station power and conveyor belts."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  sourceIds={[
    'steam-store',
    'steam-demo',
    'steam-news-2025-08-12',
    'steam-news-2026-05-28',
    'steam-news-2026-09-21',
    'steam-news-2026-10-19',
    'steam-forum-logistics',
    'steam-screens',
  ]}
  image={GAME_IMAGES['main-sequence-asteroid-mining-beams.jpg']}
  imageAlt="A Main Sequence mining ship uses several blue mining beams on nearby asteroids"
  imageCaption="The opening loop starts with the ship, not the factory. Official Steam screenshot."
  meta={['Spoiler-light', 'Demo-focused', 'Community-tested steps marked', 'Updated Nov 1, 2026']}
  faq={faq}
  pageType="WebPage"
  dataNote="This guide combines official information with one clearly labelled player-tested progression step. The game is in development; tutorial gaps may be patched."
>
  <section id="before" class={block}>
    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <p>
      Main Sequence begins at the frontier in a small mining ship. You are not
      handed a finished base, and you should not expect the opening to behave
      like a pure sandbox. The demo uses missions and research gates to introduce
      mining, station building and production.
    </p>
    <p>
      You need a Steam account and the free demo if you want to follow along
      before launch. The full game is scheduled for Early Access on November 12,
      2026. Exact recipes and costs are patch-dependent, so this guide focuses
      on the order of operations rather than numeric ratios.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <span class="callout-title">The one-sentence plan</span>
      <p class="m-0">
        Mine enough to learn the loop, build a powered station, research
        logistics, then make one complete automated production chain before
        trying to scale.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="opening" class={block}>
    <h2>The opening route</h2>
    <h3>1. Scan, mine and return with a clear purpose</h3>
    <p>
      The ship scanner identifies asteroids and their resources. Mine enough to
      test the ship workstation, but do not fill every slot blindly: the early
      goal is to understand what each ore feeds, not to hoard unknown material.
      The official store description names the scanner as the first step of the
      mining loop.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Refine and craft at the ship workstation</h3>
    <p>
      The starting ship can process ore before you build a full factory. Official
      hotfix notes reference refining enriched ores at the ship workstation, and
      the developer describes refining resources on your ship as part of the
      opening. Use this phase to learn the crafting inputs.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Build the first station room</h3>
    <p>
      The station is the center of the production game. The Command module is
      the station-design entry point. Start with a compact room that has space
      for power, a research/science machine and production. Do not build a huge
      empty hall that you cannot light or power.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Solve station power before adding machines</h3>
    <p>
      This is the most common demo roadblock. Station machines need station-side
      power. A docked ship does not automatically run the station’s research or
      production, according to a demo player report. Build the station’s
      generation and fuel path first, then place the machine that needs it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-mist">
      Status: <strong>community-tested</strong>, not an official tutorial
      statement. The current demo is based on the October 21 build; a later
      patch may improve this flow.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Research Logistics I</h3>
    <p>
      The official demo screenshot shows Logistics I in the research queue at
      0/10. Players report that conveyor belts are not available until this
      step is completed. If belts are missing from the build menu, do not
      restart: check power and the research queue first.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Build one complete loop</h3>
    <p>
      A useful first loop has a source, a processor and a destination. Once a
      machine is fed and its output is stored, you have a factory. Only then add
      another machine or a splitter. This is slower than building a huge line,
      but it makes it much easier to find the item that is stuck.
    </p>

    <Figure
      name="main-sequence-factory-floor.jpg"
      alt="A Main Sequence station factory floor with rows of orange machines, conveyor belts and railings"
      caption="Machine rows and belts take up space. Leave walking room and room for the next link in the chain."
      sizes="(min-width: 768px) 760px, 100vw"
      class="my-6"
    />

    <div class="panel p-5 not-prose">
      <p class="font-mono text-[0.59rem] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-signal">Stuck here?</p>
      <h3 class="mt-2 font-display text-2xl font-semibold uppercase text-paper">Read the focused fix</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-mist">
        Our conveyor belt guide walks through station power, the research queue
        and the specific tutorial gap in more detail.
      </p>
      <a href="/guides/first-station-logistics/" class="arrow-link mt-4">Unlock conveyor belts</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="station" class={block}>
    <h2>Station power and your first factory floor</h2>
    <p>
      Treat the first station as a small workshop, not an empire. The useful
      zones are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Power:</strong> generator, fuel supply and the machines that
        depend on it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Research:</strong> the science/research input and the queue you
        use to unlock the next tier.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Production:</strong> the first refinery or crafting machine and
        a clear route to storage.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Logistics:</strong> one belt line, with space for a future
        splitter or elevator instead of a tangle of belts.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The exact machine names and recipes can change. The durable lesson is the
      dependency: power supports research, research unlocks logistics, and
      logistics turns one machine into a production chain.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a layout method rather than a copy-paste build, read the{' '}
      <a href="/guides/factory-layout/">factory layout guide</a>. For the
      confirmed module list, the <a href="/database/modules/">modules
      database</a> separates Station, Production, Power, Logistics, Defense and
      Ship modules.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="research" class={block}>
    <h2>What to research early</h2>
    <p>
      The visible demo tree places <strong>Logistics I → Mining Drones → Computation Science → Logistics II</strong> on one automation line.
      Power is a separate branch beginning with Hydrogen Power and Solar Panels.
      The production branch begins with Silica Refining and Ore Enrichment.
    </p>

    <div class="data-table-wrap not-prose mt-5">
      <table class="data-table data-table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Early goal</th>
            <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
            <th scope="col">Confirmed context</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Early goal">Logistics I</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">Moves your base from hand-feeding to belts.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed context">Visible in the demo tree and shown at 0/10 in the official screenshot.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Early goal">Power generation</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">Machines and research stop without it.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed context">Hydrogen Power and Solar Panels are visible in the power branch.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Early goal">Mining Drones</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">Adds station-side mining automation.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed context">The developer names Mining Drone Bays as a resource-gathering path.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Early goal">Ore processing</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">Feeds construction, modules and science.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed context">Silica Refining, Ore Enrichment and Cobalt Refining are visible production nodes.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Early goal">Slipstream Drive</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">Unlocks travel around the starting system.</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Confirmed context">Steam names it as the demo progression goal; it runs only on Warp Fuel.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-mist">
      Costs are not shown for most nodes in the public screenshot. We do not
      invent a research-time ranking. See the full{' '}
      <a href="/database/research/">research database</a> for the visible branch
      order.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tips" class={block}>
    <h2>Field tips that survive patches</h2>
    <h3>Build for diagnosability</h3>
    <p>
      A machine that visibly receives items and outputs to a known container is
      easier to debug than a beautiful floor with three possible failure
      points. Add complexity after the first loop is stable.
    </p>
    <h3>Keep the research path visible</h3>
    <p>
      The research queue is your actual progression map. Before adding a new
      production line, check which science or module it feeds. If the answer is
      “nothing yet” you can probably postpone it.
    </p>
    <h3>Do not trust a borrowed ratio</h3>
    <p>
      Recipes and build costs changed during the demo period. A ratio from an
      older video or an older demo build may no longer be correct. Use the
      in-game UI and the current patch notes.
    </p>
    <h3>Use the ship as a mobile test bench</h3>
    <p>
      Early refining and manual crafting happen aboard the ship. You do not need
      to solve the station first; use the ship to learn the material chain, then
      move that process into the factory.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mistakes" class={block}>
    <h2>Common beginner mistakes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Assuming belts should be in the build menu from the start.</strong>
        They are gated behind Logistics I in the current demo.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Building machines before station power.</strong> The machine
        exists, but it will not process anything without power.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ignoring the research queue.</strong> “Missing” technology is
        often a prerequisite problem rather than a bug.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Expanding the station footprint too early.</strong> A large room
        costs more to fill and makes power and logistics harder to diagnose.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Expecting demo saves to carry over.</strong> Transfer has not
        been confirmed. Keep screenshots of designs and treat the run as a
        preview.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Using outdated build ratios.</strong> The October update and
        earlier hotfixes changed research flow, modules and balance.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="launch" class={block}>
    <h2>Demo versus Early Access</h2>
    <p>
      The demo is a limited slice. The Early Access build is expected to include
      the core progression and around 40 hours of developer-estimated content,
      with more systems planned during the Early Access period. The demo’s
      starting system and first technology tiers are not a full roadmap.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are deciding whether to start at release, the practical question is
      not “is there enough content?” but “do I want to play a game that is
      explicitly still changing?” The store page and the developer both say the
      Early Access build will evolve. If you need a fixed balance and complete
      feature set, waiting is a valid choice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check the <a href="/release-date/">release status page</a> for the date,
      price status and requirements. The page is updated when the publisher
      confirms new launch details.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
